 "transcript": "--- 20261008_C5195.MP4 (times are mm:ss in this file)\n[00:00] I'm 12 years old, I think, because I've\n[00:02] been treated by gene therapy.\n[00:05] But that was something that Bryson was tested for.\n[00:08] And we looked it up online and saw how serious it was.\n[00:11] And we saw that kids were dying before they were two.\n[00:15] And we had to wait for three months\n[00:17] for the tests to come back.\n[00:19] And after four or five months, my wife\n[00:22] called up the children's hospital\n[00:25] and said, shouldn't we be getting our tests back by now?\n[00:27] Like they said, three months.\n[00:29] The person on the phone, I think it was a nurse,\n[00:32] was like, no, no, we'll call you when they come in.\n[00:34] Don't worry.\n[00:35] And a couple more months went by, and she called again.\n[00:38] And she looked into it and realized\n[00:40] that the tests had never actually been sent out.\n--- 20261008_C5194.MP4 (times are mm:ss in this file)\n[00:00] the actual cause.\n[00:02] So that kind of led to what often gets\n[00:05] referred to as a diagnostic odyssey.\n[00:08] And ours was long.\n[00:09] So Bryson didn't get his diagnosis\n[00:11] till he was almost 10 years old.\n[00:14] We had a couple of MRIs.\n[00:18] He had a biopsy to try to determine\n[00:20] if the low muscle tone was in his muscles or in his brain.\n[00:23] And his muscles turned out to be normal.\n[00:25] His brain looked normal on the MRI.\n[00:28] So we did a number of different first individual tests\n[00:32] for specific disorders, then for panels.\n[00:39] But what ultimately gave us the diagnosis\n[00:41] was a whole exome sequence.\n[00:43] And he didn't get till he was almost 10.\n[00:45] And for each test that they did, it\n[00:49] caused so much fear for my wife and I.\n[00:54] And so one example is.\n--- 20261008_C5197.MP4 (times are mm:ss in this file)\n[00:00] in Toronto and so he continues to be a physician for Bryson,\n[00:06] but he's also someone very, obviously,\n[00:08] very important to that system.\n[00:12] So eventually we got this call, my wife got a call\n[00:15] that they wanted to set up an appointment with us\n[00:18] because they had found something in the genetics report\n[00:21] and the person who called actually was the genetic counselor\n[00:25] and she said, well can you tell us what it is?\n[00:27] She's like, no, let's wait for the appointment,\n[00:28] it'll be the geneticist who tells you what it is,\n[00:31] but I'll be there.\n[00:32] And my wife just said, well can you tell us,\n[00:34] is it degenerative?\n[00:35] So the key thing we really wanted to know\n[00:38] is what she meant by that is,\n[00:39] is Bryson gonna die from this?\n[00:41] Is he gonna die from this soon?\n[00:43] And she basically just said, we don't think so.\n[00:45] So we both felt quite relieved to learn that.\n--- 20261008_C5198.MP4 (times are mm:ss in this file)\n[00:00] were a handful of patients who had been diagnosed.\n[00:04] And so on the way home, usually my wife always likes to drive.\n[00:07] She likes to be in control, so she drives.\n[00:09] But this day, she asked me to drive.\n[00:11] And so she was googling GRIN1.\n[00:14] And at the time, these were the top results\n[00:16] that came up, which were all publications about GRIN1\n[00:20] related to schizophrenia.\n[00:21] So we were like, is this connected to schizophrenia?\n[00:25] Because there had been very few publications at the time\n[00:27] about it being a monogenic disorder.\n[00:32] So from that, we found a Facebook group that at the time\n[00:36] had six other families.\n[00:38] We were the seventh to join this Facebook group of GRIN1\n[00:40] families around the world.\n[00:44] Now there are hundreds in that group."
}
